				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/werdna" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(2161854); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=2161854&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1605795250" alt="werdna" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=2161854)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/werdna" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(2161854); return false;" >werdna</a></span> <span class="odate time_1530513325 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">02 Jul 2018 06:35</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-3858360">
									

<p>To say SETI are idiots for using what is nearly the simplest possible system for encoding information in order to make it as easy as possible for recipients to decode a message is just plain silly. It's not flawed to think aliens would understand a simple system like binary (for that matter, it doesn't make any sense to say binary is something that would be &quot;rendered obsolete&quot;). You're looking at a simple, universal, mathematical concept like binary (referring to a system with two possible states, like the Arecibo message, rather than a binary place-value system) like it's some kind of clumsily developed man-made technology, rather than a simple mathematical concept, like addition.</p>
<p>I liked the SCP though.</p>

