					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/rigen" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(2826105); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=2826105&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1605795250" alt="Rigen" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=2826105)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/rigen" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(2826105); return false;" >Rigen</a></span> <span class="odate time_1530513846 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">02 Jul 2018 06:44</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-3858376">
									

<p>To add over that: even if we know that the aliens could somehow render binary &quot;obsolete&quot; how could we even know what they're using? Like it or not we're still trapped in von Neumann architecture. Quantum computing has only been available in last decade. DNA computing is still far off&#8212;and without guarantee aliens would even have DNA. Betting on something that we ourselves haven't used species-wide is more of an oxymoron than betting on the building blocks that let us build the first switch all the way to nano-scale transistor. I think the Arecibo message is not be all end all messages, but a stepping stone to determine if there's someone out there to listen, and then we could start talking with more sophisticated method. It's a baby's cry before the parents come and teach them proper language.</p>
<p>There's a good reason why Morse code is so prevalent and successful.</p>

							</div>

								
