				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/dankaar" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1790336); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=1790336&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1605795250" alt="dankaar" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=1790336)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/dankaar" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1790336); return false;" >dankaar</a></span> <span class="odate time_1544660941 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">13 Dec 2018 00:29</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4084635">
									

<p>It's not just that the system is binary. It's also the encoding in it. Without knowing the encryption method used, a binary message will just look like an ordered pattern without context.</p>
<p>A good example of what I mean is morse code. It is effectively binary: a sound and silence. Without knowing what they represent, all you are hearing is a pattern of sounds and silences. With some analysis, you could determine the two states of sound, and that the silence indicates a grouping of long and short sounds.</p>
<p>But then you hit that roadblock. It's clearly artificial, and it likely means something, but what? Lacking knowledge of its encryption, meaning its context, it's virtually impossible to gain any meaningful information from it.</p>

							</div>

								
					
