					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/decibelles" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(2005044); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=2005044&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1605795250" alt="Decibelles" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=2005044)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/decibelles" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(2005044); return false;" >Decibelles</a></span> <span class="odate time_1530478219 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">01 Jul 2018 20:50</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-3857788">
									

<p>The more I read this, the more I don't enjoy it.</p>
<p>I get what you're aiming for here, but I'm having a hard time being able to suspend my disbelief for this. While you introduce background for the setting this takes place in, I have a hard time being able to buy into the premise to begin with. The Foundation's long-gone, and this version of them documenting the Foundation in a unique way, as well as the build-up to the Destroyer, ultimately makes me wish I was just reading this from the actual Foundation's perspective instead of a retrospective. (Also, I understand why you used Stellar Congressional Protectorate but I really wish you came up with a name that didn't fit that acronym to fully go-in on your format screw.)</p>
<p>The pacing suffers from this. The use of displaying pictographs, explaining it, and then analyzing it gets exhausting after a while, and it feels like a lot of hoops to jump through in order to get into the meat of the story, which I don't find interesting if we're not seeing it directly unfold from the main viewpoint of the Foundation. At most, it's a curious read for a story that I don't feel pays off. Not to mention, I'm tired of planet-ending monstrous threats in SCP articles.</p>
<p>I initially liked it because of how different it felt with the format screw, but it doesn't pay off for me, and the more I reflect on it, the more I can't get behind it.</p>

							</div>

								
					
